# fixedbugs, M-header-constants, 2026-08-12.
#
# SYMPTOM: the language has no mutable globals (§4.2:673), and `constant stdout:
# ptr` is one. On this platform `stdout` is `#define stdout __stdoutp` over an
# `extern FILE *`, and `errno` is `(*__error())` — both are *objects*, so a
# zero-argument accessor over one returns whatever it holds **at the time of the
# call**. Two reads, two answers, from a declaration that says `constant`.
#
# Predicted by panel 038's historian before the form existed, and it named the
# direction: without a check, Heroes' mechanism is *more* permissive than Zig's —
# `errno` and `stdout` would compile, link, run and exit 0 with zero diagnostics,
# because the preprocessor resolves them into perfectly valid expressions. A
# failure in the quiet direction (CLAUDE.md §11).
#
# CAUSE: the type assertion asks what type the header gives a name, and both of
# these have the right type. The question it does not ask is whether the header
# gives it a **value**.
#
# FIX: `_Static_assert(__builtin_constant_p(X), "…")` per constant. The builtin is
# itself a constant expression even when its argument is not, so the failure stays
# an assertion carrying *our* message — which is what keeps `emit/ffi.rs` matching
# only what this emitter writes. The alternative measured by the ffi-pragmatist, a
# `static const T probe = X;` initializer, fails with clang's own words and would
# have widened CLAUDE.md §7's named exception from a message to a generated line.
#
# Heroes appears to be the first language to refuse this as a language rule: Nim,
# Swift and Go all accept naming a C object, and Zig refuses it by accident, as a
# comptime-evaluation failure its own tracker calls a bug (sourced, panel 038).
#
# This file must NOT compile. It is built by the CLI surface test
# `a_c_object_is_not_a_constant`.

extern "stdio.h"
    constant stdout: ptr  #~ ffi_not_constant

function main()
    print(stdout == nullptr)
